docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:314:  > **Pre-surgery preparation** Your surgeon will explain your procedure and individual recovery plan, and you may need to follow a specific pre-op diet to shrink your liver and reduce surgical risk (typically 2–4 weeks). But you won’t do it alone! Our staff will support you throughout.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:316:  > **Procedure day** Obesity Surgery WA’s bariatric surgery is performed via keyhole surgery in most cases, with short post-operative hospital stays (1-3 nights) at [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth. Guided by nursing and dietetic staff, you’ll start on fluids and your recovery will be monitored until you’re ready to return home.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:318:  > **Long-term aftercare** Most light daily activities can usually be resumed gradually from around the ~2-3 week mark, with return to work depending on which procedure you have and what type of work you do (~2+ weeks’ for desk work/4+ weeks for physical or manual labour). Your post-op progression from fluids only to regular textures will take place with the support of your dietitian. Plus you’ll need ongoing reviews (regularly post-op then annually) and bloodwork done to protect your nutrition and results long-term.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:322:  > 1. **Initial enquiry and GP referral:** most patients start with a GP referral, which can help with Medicare and private health requirements; if you contact OSWA directly, the team can guide you on what is needed. 2. **Consultation with your surgeon:** your surgeon reviews your history, goals and health conditions, then explains which surgical or non-surgical pathways may be appropriate. 3. **Wider team assessment:** this may include physician, dietitian and exercise physiology input. 4. **Pre-surgery preparation:** you receive procedure-specific preparation advice, including any pre-operative diet. 5. **Procedure day:** surgery is usually laparoscopic, with hospital stay and recovery guidance tailored to the procedure. 6. **Long-term aftercare:** follow-up, nutrition monitoring and habit support continue after surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:326:  > **Gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy)**: our most common procedure and suitable for a wide range of patients. A large portion of the stomach is removed, leaving a slim tube (the ‘sleeve’) through which food moves much more slowly, and reduces ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’). Many people lose ~60–70% of excess weight, though individual results will vary.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:328:  > **Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y)**: restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70–80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:330:  > **Mini gastric bypass (one anastomosis bypass)**: a simplified variation of the Roux-en-Y bypass that creates a single connection between the stomach and small intestine, rather than two. The result is a shorter, less complex operation with a similar weight loss outcome for most patients, though with a slightly different risk profile. Your surgeon can advise whether it's a suitable option for your circumstances.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:336:  > **Medical weight management**: non-surgical weight loss treatment pathways including GLP-1 medications (such as semaglutide) and structured dietitian-led programs. Suits people who do not want surgery, are not yet candidates, or who would like specialist medical support before or instead of proceeding with a surgical procedure.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:357:  > Eating habits will change — smaller portions, slower eating, and in some cases avoiding certain foods, particularly in the early months following surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:371:- **Replace the seven-answer FAQ block** (source lines 270-342):
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:377:  > **2. Is bariatric surgery available on Medicare or private health insurance?** Medicare rebates apply to most bariatric procedures when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance with a gold-tier or equivalent policy covers the hospital and theatre component. The combination of both typically means a significant portion of the total cost is covered. Out-of-pocket costs vary depending on your level of cover, excess, and anaesthetist fees. Our team provides a clear, itemised cost breakdown before any commitment is made, so you know exactly where you stand.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:381:  > **4. Do I have to have surgery, or are there non-surgical options?** Surgery is not the only path. Medical weight management, including GLP-1 medications and dietitian-led programs, is a genuine and effective option for many people, and our team offers this alongside surgical pathways. Some patients pursue medical management first and decide to progress to surgery later; others find it sufficient on its own. The goal is the right outcome for you, not a particular procedure.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:383:  > **5. What does aftercare involve, and why does it matter so much?** Aftercare typically includes follow-up appointments with your surgeon, ongoing dietitian and exercise physiology support, nutritional monitoring via blood tests, and guidance on building sustainable habits. It matters because surgery changes your body, but long-term results are shaped by what happens in the months and years that follow. Patients who stay engaged with their care team consistently tend to achieve better long-term outcomes. At Obesity Surgery WA, we view your surgery as the beginning of your weight loss journey, not the end.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:385:  > **6. Where will my surgery and appointments take place?** Consultations take place at our clinics in Booragoon or Greenfields, with surgeries performed at [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth. Once your surgery is planned, our team will walk you through exactly what to expect, where to go, and who will be involved at every stage, so there are no surprises on the day.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:399:Replace the existing FAQ heading and answers (source lines 270-342) with:
